1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
Imagine you are standing on a runway
somewhere in the Canadian Arctic.

2
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:09,420
It is freezing. There is no major
airport nearby. No repair centre.

3
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,220
No endless supply of spare parts.

4
00:00:13,039 --> 00:00:14,939
And then an airplane arrives.

5
00:00:15,537 --> 00:00:17,717
It has two giant radial engines.

6
00:00:18,577 --> 00:00:24,237
Its cockpit is full of round mechanical
instruments. No screens. No fly-by-wire.

7
00:00:24,317 --> 00:00:25,517
No computers.

8
00:00:26,091 --> 00:00:28,321
This is the Douglas DC-3.

9
00:00:28,731 --> 00:00:30,911
It first flew in 1935.

10
00:00:32,098 --> 00:00:36,678
During the Second World War, more than
ten thousand military versions were built.

11
00:00:36,993 --> 00:00:38,853
And more than ninety
years after that first

12
00:00:39,083 --> 00:00:41,533
flight, some of them are still working.

13
00:00:42,145 --> 00:00:46,885
Not in a museum. Hauling cargo
and supplies to remote places.

14
00:00:47,683 --> 00:00:51,323
Most airliners are retired
after about twenty-five years.

15
00:00:52,063 --> 00:00:55,863
Some DC-3s still at work are
more than three times that age.

16
00:00:56,570 --> 00:00:59,250
So what did Douglas get
so incredibly right?

17
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:06,050
To understand that, we need to go back
to the early days of passenger flying.

18
00:01:06,789 --> 00:01:10,989
Airliners were noisy. They were
slow. They were uncomfortable.

19
00:01:11,329 --> 00:01:15,169
And many airlines couldn't make
money carrying passengers alone.

20
00:01:15,689 --> 00:01:18,409
They depended on mail
contracts and subsidies.

21
00:01:19,085 --> 00:01:20,518
Then Douglas built the DC-2.

22
00:01:20,748 --> 00:01:22,765
All-metal construction.

23
00:01:23,425 --> 00:01:25,125
Retractable landing gear.

24
00:01:25,605 --> 00:01:28,065
A streamlined fuselage. Twin engines.

25
00:01:28,649 --> 00:01:30,409
And then Douglas took what worked...

26
00:01:30,489 --> 00:01:35,369
and made it bigger, more comfortable and
more capable. The result was the DC-3.

27
00:01:36,547 --> 00:01:41,847
And suddenly, passenger flying made
economic sense. It was fast enough.

28
00:01:42,247 --> 00:01:45,347
It carried enough people.
It had enough range.

29
00:01:45,427 --> 00:01:47,047
And it was reliable enough.

30
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,840
For the first time, an airline could
make money carrying passengers alone.

31
00:01:52,760 --> 00:01:55,860
The DC-3 didn't just become
a successful airplane.

32
00:01:56,300 --> 00:01:59,440
It helped turn air travel
into a real industry.

33
00:01:59,952 --> 00:02:03,532
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

34
00:02:04,252 --> 00:02:05,832
It really helps the channel.

35
00:02:06,871 --> 00:02:09,371
So what was the secret? There wasn't one.

36
00:02:09,951 --> 00:02:11,651
And that's the interesting part.

37
00:02:12,345 --> 00:02:15,665
The DC-3 didn't have
one magical technology.

38
00:02:16,145 --> 00:02:19,165
Almost everything about it
was good at the same time.

39
00:02:19,482 --> 00:02:22,562
And above all, it was good at the
one thing an airliner has to do.

40
00:02:22,642 --> 00:02:23,562
Make money.

41
00:02:23,792 --> 00:02:26,062
Douglas made the cabin wider.

42
00:02:26,292 --> 00:02:29,342
Twenty-one seats instead of fourteen.

43
00:02:29,582 --> 00:02:32,122
Fifty percent more seats, on every flight.

44
00:02:32,811 --> 00:02:36,591
It wasn't the fastest airliner.
It wasn't the biggest.

45
00:02:37,151 --> 00:02:39,091
It didn't have the longest range.

46
00:02:39,171 --> 00:02:42,731
But it was good enough at
almost everything that mattered.

47
00:02:43,387 --> 00:02:45,987
Its wings were relatively
large for its weight.

48
00:02:46,747 --> 00:02:50,527
That let it fly slowly and safely,
and use short, rough runways.

49
00:02:51,067 --> 00:02:52,567
Its structure was strong.

50
00:02:53,247 --> 00:02:54,867
Its systems were simple.

51
00:02:55,179 --> 00:02:58,819
And with two engines, if one failed,
the other could keep it flying.

52
00:02:58,899 --> 00:03:00,819
Not indefinitely.

53
00:03:01,299 --> 00:03:03,639
But long enough to give
the pilots options.

54
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:08,620
That matters enormously when the nearest
airport is hundreds of kilometres away.

55
00:03:10,690 --> 00:03:12,570
And then there was the way it flew.

56
00:03:13,210 --> 00:03:16,490
Pilots describe the DC-3
as stable and predictable.

57
00:03:17,037 --> 00:03:18,957
If you were slightly
too slow, the airplane

58
00:03:19,187 --> 00:03:21,577
warned you before things got really bad.

59
00:03:21,868 --> 00:03:26,008
And a small movement of the controls
gave you a small, smooth response.

60
00:03:26,588 --> 00:03:28,208
Never a sudden lurch.

61
00:03:28,899 --> 00:03:30,559
It even talked to you.

62
00:03:31,099 --> 00:03:33,179
Some veteran airline
pilots claimed they could

63
00:03:33,409 --> 00:03:35,879
feel a stewardess walking
up the aisle with coffee.

64
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:40,319
Her weight shifted the balance,
and they felt it in the controls.

65
00:03:41,202 --> 00:03:43,142
Landing was gentle, too.

66
00:03:43,662 --> 00:03:46,242
Those big wings settled
softly as the airplane neared

67
00:03:46,322 --> 00:03:49,442
the ground, and it had
little tendency to bounce.

68
00:03:50,162 --> 00:03:54,342
Some pilots say it's easier to land
than many small private airplanes.

69
00:03:55,697 --> 00:03:58,697
Pilots have a word for that. Forgiving.

70
00:04:00,043 --> 00:04:04,783
And think about where the DC-3
spent its working life. Bad weather.

71
00:04:05,143 --> 00:04:08,243
Short gravel strips. Long days.

72
00:04:08,623 --> 00:04:11,363
Pilots who were tired,
or still new to the job.

73
00:04:11,923 --> 00:04:14,103
An airplane that punishes
every small mistake

74
00:04:14,333 --> 00:04:16,543
doesn't last long in that kind of life.

75
00:04:17,303 --> 00:04:19,003
One that forgives them might.

76
00:04:19,701 --> 00:04:22,241
It doesn't mean the DC-3 couldn't crash.

77
00:04:23,101 --> 00:04:24,641
It absolutely could.

78
00:04:25,261 --> 00:04:29,201
It means small mistakes didn't
immediately turn into catastrophic ones.

79
00:04:29,281 --> 00:04:33,101
And in aviation, that room
to recover can be priceless.

80
00:04:33,947 --> 00:04:37,827
As one DC-3 pilot put it, it's
slow to get you into trouble...

81
00:04:38,007 --> 00:04:40,287
but even slower to get you out of it.

82
00:04:42,707 --> 00:04:44,687
But here's the really weird part.

83
00:04:45,147 --> 00:04:48,407
Nobody in 1935 set out
to build an airplane

84
00:04:48,637 --> 00:04:51,547
that would still be flying in 2026.

85
00:04:52,027 --> 00:04:54,667
The goal was simply a
really good airliner.

86
00:04:55,157 --> 00:04:58,517
But some of the designers'
choices aged remarkably well.

87
00:04:59,137 --> 00:05:03,877
An aluminium structure. Mechanical
controls. Mechanical engines.

88
00:05:04,729 --> 00:05:08,749
A mechanic could understand most of
the machine just by looking at it.

89
00:05:09,389 --> 00:05:11,649
And that becomes extremely important when

90
00:05:11,879 --> 00:05:14,269
your airplane is more
than eighty years old.

91
00:05:14,633 --> 00:05:17,393
When something breaks on a
modern aircraft, you may need

92
00:05:17,623 --> 00:05:20,673
special diagnostic equipment,
a replacement electronic

93
00:05:20,903 --> 00:05:24,933
module, software, and someone
certified to work on it.

94
00:05:25,481 --> 00:05:28,081
Now imagine doing that in the Arctic.

95
00:05:28,798 --> 00:05:33,018
That's where the DC-3's old-fashioned
design becomes an advantage.

96
00:05:33,558 --> 00:05:37,938
Picture one on a gravel strip, hundreds
of kilometres from the nearest hangar.

97
00:05:38,698 --> 00:05:40,198
Something isn't working.

98
00:05:40,667 --> 00:05:45,547
A mechanic can often open a panel and
actually see the problem. A cable.

99
00:05:46,187 --> 00:05:51,067
A pulley. A leaking line. No
error code. Just the part itself.

100
00:05:51,607 --> 00:05:56,347
And generations of mechanics have learned
how to keep these airplanes alive.

101
00:05:56,708 --> 00:05:58,288
That doesn't make it easy.

102
00:05:59,008 --> 00:06:03,548
The problem is no longer understanding
the machine. It's finding parts.

103
00:06:03,628 --> 00:06:05,348
Finding specialists.

104
00:06:05,528 --> 00:06:07,848
And satisfying modern aviation rules.

105
00:06:08,522 --> 00:06:10,182
The airplane may be simple.

106
00:06:10,262 --> 00:06:12,582
The world around it isn't.

107
00:06:14,852 --> 00:06:18,552
The DC-3 also arrived at
exactly the right moment.

108
00:06:19,132 --> 00:06:22,052
Aerodynamics, aluminium
construction and aircraft

109
00:06:22,282 --> 00:06:24,932
engines had all matured at the same time.

110
00:06:25,672 --> 00:06:27,932
And then history gave it an enormous push.

111
00:06:28,605 --> 00:06:32,525
During the war, the military
version was called the C-47.

112
00:06:32,985 --> 00:06:36,025
That's where most of those
ten thousand came from.

113
00:06:36,533 --> 00:06:39,093
They carried troops, cargo,
paratroopers and medical

114
00:06:39,323 --> 00:06:43,333
supplies, across enormous
distances, in brutal conditions.

115
00:06:44,045 --> 00:06:47,805
And suddenly there were thousands of
people who knew how to fly them...

116
00:06:47,985 --> 00:06:50,545
and thousands more who
knew how to fix them.

117
00:06:51,031 --> 00:06:55,011
After the war, many of those
aircraft were sold for civilian use.

118
00:06:55,691 --> 00:06:58,311
And the DC-3 stopped
being just an airplane.

119
00:06:58,931 --> 00:07:00,531
It became an ecosystem.

120
00:07:01,457 --> 00:07:03,857
Parts. Mechanics. Pilots.
Training. Experience.

121
00:07:04,087 --> 00:07:05,597
And airplanes everywhere.

122
00:07:08,167 --> 00:07:11,407
Sometimes a machine survives
not because it is perfect...

123
00:07:12,524 --> 00:07:15,504
but because so many people
know how to keep it alive.

124
00:07:18,734 --> 00:07:20,954
So why aren't we still
using them everywhere?

125
00:07:21,674 --> 00:07:24,654
Because being durable isn't
the same as being efficient.

126
00:07:25,445 --> 00:07:28,385
A modern turboprop carries
more, burns less fuel for

127
00:07:28,615 --> 00:07:31,625
every passenger, and has
far more capable avionics.

128
00:07:32,525 --> 00:07:34,065
And fuel isn't free.

129
00:07:34,585 --> 00:07:36,105
Neither is maintenance.

130
00:07:36,937 --> 00:07:39,237
So why would anyone still use one?

131
00:07:40,271 --> 00:07:41,811
Because efficiency isn't everything.

132
00:07:42,041 --> 00:07:43,591
Remote areas.

133
00:07:43,821 --> 00:07:45,351
Rough airfields.

134
00:07:45,581 --> 00:07:47,531
Specialised cargo work.

135
00:07:47,987 --> 00:07:50,687
Places where being able to
operate independently matters

136
00:07:50,917 --> 00:07:53,727
more than squeezing out the
last percent of efficiency.

137
00:07:56,537 --> 00:07:59,177
Some DC-3s have even been rebuilt.

138
00:07:59,897 --> 00:08:02,697
The radial engines
replaced with turboprops.

139
00:08:03,177 --> 00:08:05,397
The old cockpit with modern avionics.

140
00:08:06,215 --> 00:08:09,535
One famous example is the Basler BT-67.

141
00:08:10,235 --> 00:08:15,035
It starts with a DC-3 airframe,
and replaces much of what's inside.

142
00:08:15,828 --> 00:08:18,268
It's like taking an
eighty-year-old classic car...

143
00:08:18,498 --> 00:08:22,048
and fitting it with a modern
engine and modern electronics.

144
00:08:22,857 --> 00:08:25,437
The body survives. The concept survives.

145
00:08:26,017 --> 00:08:27,837
The technology around it changes.

146
00:08:30,337 --> 00:08:34,117
We often imagine technological
progress as a straight line.

147
00:08:34,717 --> 00:08:38,417
New technology replaces the old,
and the old stuff disappears.

148
00:08:39,373 --> 00:08:43,173
But sometimes, an old design gets
something fundamentally right.

149
00:08:43,933 --> 00:08:46,573
And then newer technology
doesn't destroy it.

150
00:08:47,013 --> 00:08:48,513
It gets added to it.

151
00:08:49,052 --> 00:08:52,372
The DC-3 doesn't have to
compete with a modern airliner.

152
00:08:53,252 --> 00:08:56,952
It just has to keep doing the
job it was exceptionally good at.

153
00:08:57,491 --> 00:08:59,391
And apparently, it is very difficult to

154
00:08:59,621 --> 00:09:02,031
convince this particular
airplane to retire.

155
00:09:04,011 --> 00:09:06,311
So somewhere in the
Arctic, more than ninety

156
00:09:06,541 --> 00:09:09,471
years after the first
DC-3 took to the sky...

157
00:09:09,966 --> 00:09:11,546
one of them may be starting its engines.

158
00:09:11,776 --> 00:09:14,556
And flying to work, like
it has for generations.
